---
description: Project preference - do not write tests or code comments
alwaysApply: true
---

# No Tests

Do not create test files, test functions, or test fixtures. Do not add testing
dependencies or test runner configuration.

Verify work by running the code itself (start the server, hit the endpoint, run the
script) instead of writing a test for it.

Only write tests when the user explicitly asks for them in that request.

# No Comments

Do not add comments to code. This includes docstrings, inline comments, block comments,
and TODO notes.

```python
# ❌ BAD
async def create(self, payload: ItemCreate) -> ItemRead:
    """Create an item and return it."""
    # acquire the lock so IDs stay unique
    async with self._lock:
        ...

# ✅ GOOD
async def create(self, payload: ItemCreate) -> ItemRead:
    async with self._lock:
        ...
```

Write self-explanatory names instead of explanatory comments. Preserve comments that
already exist in files you edit; this rule governs what you add, not what is there.

Exceptions, which are not comments in the prose sense: license headers, shebangs, coding
declarations, pragmas that change tooling behavior (`# type: ignore`, `# noqa`,
`# pyright: ignore`), and comments the user explicitly requests.
